1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,710
Meet Maya.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,490
She lives in Northern Virginia,

3
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:05,520
and this is her electric bill.

4
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,697
About about a hundred and fifty dollars

5
00:00:08,697 --> 00:00:09,640
a month.

6
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,650
Now look at her new neighbors.

7
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:12,743
Giant windowless buildings,

8
00:00:12,743 --> 00:00:13,678
packed with AI chips,

9
00:00:13,678 --> 00:00:15,070
humming day and night.

10
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:16,064
This summer,

11
00:00:16,064 --> 00:00:17,794
the grid that powers her street bought

12
00:00:17,794 --> 00:00:20,390
its future electricity at the highest

13
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:21,882
price the rules allow,

14
00:00:21,882 --> 00:00:23,780
and it still came up short.

15
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:25,779
The grid's own watchdog says those

16
00:00:25,779 --> 00:00:28,400
buildings are a big reason why.

17
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,400
So who really pays for the AI

18
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:31,210
boom?

19
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,550
The tech giants...

20
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,926
or Maya?

21
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:47,323
Here's what those buildings eat.

22
00:00:47,463 --> 00:00:50,485
A data center turns electricity into

23
00:00:50,485 --> 00:00:51,159
answers,

24
00:00:51,159 --> 00:00:51,833
and heat,

25
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:53,353
around the clock.

26
00:00:53,353 --> 00:00:55,509
Government researchers at Berkeley Lab

27
00:00:55,509 --> 00:00:56,893
measured it.

28
00:00:56,983 --> 00:00:58,169
In their latest count,

29
00:00:58,169 --> 00:01:00,985
America's data centers were already using

30
00:01:00,985 --> 00:01:03,599
as much electricity as about seventeen

31
00:01:03,599 --> 00:01:05,003
million homes.

32
00:01:05,113 --> 00:01:07,364
And their forecast says that could nearly

33
00:01:07,364 --> 00:01:08,017
double,

34
00:01:08,017 --> 00:01:09,344
or even triple,

35
00:01:09,344 --> 00:01:11,520
before the decade is out.

36
00:01:11,783 --> 00:01:12,743
Maya doesn't hate AI.

37
00:01:12,743 --> 00:01:16,683
Her kids use it for homework.

38
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:19,043
She uses it to plan dinners.

39
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:22,196
But every one of those questions travels

40
00:01:22,196 --> 00:01:24,296
to a building like this one,

41
00:01:24,296 --> 00:01:26,688
and that building pulls power from the

42
00:01:26,688 --> 00:01:28,255
same grid as her fridge,

43
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:31,513
her air conditioner and her porch light.

44
00:01:31,753 --> 00:01:34,493
Which raises a simple question.

45
00:01:34,653 --> 00:01:36,401
When the grid has to grow for

46
00:01:36,401 --> 00:01:37,125
the giants,

47
00:01:37,125 --> 00:01:39,223
who pays for the growing?

48
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:41,273
Let me draw it.

49
00:01:41,403 --> 00:01:43,318
Each little house is one home's

50
00:01:43,318 --> 00:01:45,833
electricity for a year.

51
00:01:46,183 --> 00:01:47,129
Stack them up,

52
00:01:47,129 --> 00:01:50,042
and today's data centers already make a

53
00:01:50,042 --> 00:01:52,583
tower the size of a big state.

54
00:01:53,013 --> 00:01:55,073
Now add the forecast.

55
00:01:55,073 --> 00:01:56,759
That's our arithmetic,

56
00:01:56,759 --> 00:01:57,467
not theirs,

57
00:01:57,467 --> 00:01:59,533
but it shows the scale.

58
00:01:59,903 --> 00:02:02,483
AI isn't one more appliance.

59
00:02:02,483 --> 00:02:04,504
It's like a whole new country of

60
00:02:04,504 --> 00:02:05,155
homes,

61
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:08,000
plugging into the same wires as Maya.

62
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:10,650
Grid operators see it coming.

63
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:12,806
PJM runs the grid for Maya's state

64
00:02:12,806 --> 00:02:15,100
and several others.

65
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:16,367
A few years ago,

66
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:18,413
it expected demand on the hottest

67
00:02:18,413 --> 00:02:20,650
afternoons to barely grow.

68
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:22,966
Now it expects it to grow about

69
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:24,314
twelve times as fast,

70
00:02:24,314 --> 00:02:26,970
mainly because of data centers.

71
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:28,367
These are forecasts,

72
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:30,284
and some of these projects will never

73
00:02:30,284 --> 00:02:31,270
be built.

74
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:32,570
Remember that.

75
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:34,763
It turns out to matter a lot.

76
00:02:39,217 --> 00:02:42,027
So let's follow Maya's bill.

77
00:02:42,177 --> 00:02:44,169
The biggest slice pays for the energy

78
00:02:44,169 --> 00:02:45,067
itself.

79
00:02:45,067 --> 00:02:47,099
Another pays for capacity,

80
00:02:47,099 --> 00:02:48,945
power plants paid to promise they'll show

81
00:02:48,945 --> 00:02:51,187
up on the hottest afternoon.

82
00:02:51,367 --> 00:02:53,360
Another pays for transmission,

83
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,527
the big high-voltage lines.

84
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:57,629
The rest pays for the poles and

85
00:02:57,629 --> 00:02:59,069
wires on her street,

86
00:02:59,069 --> 00:03:00,157
plus fees.

87
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,397
And data centers can push on every

88
00:03:02,397 --> 00:03:03,974
one of those slices.

89
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:05,135
Start upstream,

90
00:03:05,135 --> 00:03:07,231
in the wholesale market,

91
00:03:07,231 --> 00:03:09,821
where utilities buy power before it

92
00:03:09,821 --> 00:03:11,127
reaches Maya.

93
00:03:11,297 --> 00:03:12,615
So far this year,

94
00:03:12,615 --> 00:03:15,574
the all-in wholesale cost in PJM is

95
00:03:15,574 --> 00:03:18,057
up about fifty percent.

96
00:03:18,317 --> 00:03:20,672
A brutal winter storm explains part of

97
00:03:20,672 --> 00:03:21,487
that.

98
00:03:21,487 --> 00:03:24,012
But the capacity slice alone roughly

99
00:03:24,012 --> 00:03:25,071
tripled.

100
00:03:25,317 --> 00:03:28,217
So the grid's watchdog asked a sharper

101
00:03:28,217 --> 00:03:29,397
question.

102
00:03:29,827 --> 00:03:31,605
What would that cost have been without

103
00:03:31,605 --> 00:03:33,317
the data centers?

104
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:35,549
Put its estimate in Maya's terms,

105
00:03:35,549 --> 00:03:37,720
and if every cent were passed straight

106
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:38,566
through to her,

107
00:03:38,566 --> 00:03:41,337
that's about eleven dollars a month.

108
00:03:41,577 --> 00:03:43,957
It usually isn't passed through in full,

109
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:45,867
and utilities hedge.

110
00:03:45,977 --> 00:03:47,655
But it isn't pennies.

111
00:03:47,917 --> 00:03:50,611
How that capacity auction works,

112
00:03:50,611 --> 00:03:52,762
and why its price first jumped,

113
00:03:52,762 --> 00:03:54,939
is the story we told in Why

114
00:03:54,939 --> 00:03:59,077
America's Electricity Bills Are Rising.

115
00:03:59,267 --> 00:04:01,227
Here's what happened next.

116
00:04:05,683 --> 00:04:06,959
Every year,

117
00:04:06,959 --> 00:04:08,496
PJM holds an auction,

118
00:04:08,496 --> 00:04:10,840
paying power plants to promise they'll be

119
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:13,263
there when the grid needs them most.

120
00:04:13,493 --> 00:04:14,810
A few years ago,

121
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:16,973
that promise was a bargain.

122
00:04:17,143 --> 00:04:18,002
This July,

123
00:04:18,002 --> 00:04:20,423
it cleared at the maximum the rules

124
00:04:20,423 --> 00:04:21,062
allow,

125
00:04:21,062 --> 00:04:23,643
more than eleven times higher.

126
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:25,629
And that maximum is a cap,

127
00:04:25,629 --> 00:04:27,169
set with the governors,

128
00:04:27,169 --> 00:04:29,554
to stop it going even higher.

129
00:04:29,817 --> 00:04:32,387
Think of it like concert tickets.

130
00:04:32,437 --> 00:04:34,557
The seats are power plants.

131
00:04:34,557 --> 00:04:36,165
The crowd is everyone who needs

132
00:04:36,165 --> 00:04:38,576
electricity on the hottest day of the

133
00:04:38,576 --> 00:04:39,377
year.

134
00:04:39,647 --> 00:04:41,978
For years the crowd barely grew,

135
00:04:41,978 --> 00:04:43,887
and tickets were cheap.

136
00:04:44,027 --> 00:04:45,826
Then a new group showed up at

137
00:04:45,826 --> 00:04:46,928
the box office,

138
00:04:46,928 --> 00:04:49,177
with very deep pockets and a very

139
00:04:49,177 --> 00:04:50,677
big appetite.

140
00:04:50,837 --> 00:04:52,745
When the venue can't add seats fast

141
00:04:52,745 --> 00:04:53,535
enough,

142
00:04:53,535 --> 00:04:55,988
the price goes up for everyone in

143
00:04:55,988 --> 00:04:56,860
line.

144
00:04:57,117 --> 00:04:58,938
And even at the cap,

145
00:04:58,938 --> 00:05:01,050
the auction came up short of what

146
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:02,897
PJM says it needs to keep the

147
00:05:02,897 --> 00:05:04,817
lights reliably on.

148
00:05:04,857 --> 00:05:06,800
It's the second auction in a row

149
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,506
where the whole grid fell short,

150
00:05:08,506 --> 00:05:10,617
a first in its history.

151
00:05:10,847 --> 00:05:13,604
Demand is arriving faster than steel and

152
00:05:13,604 --> 00:05:14,705
concrete.

153
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:18,140
So how much of this is AI?

154
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:19,450
The market monitor estimates that across

155
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:20,174
its last several auctions,

156
00:05:20,174 --> 00:05:21,181
data centers were behind almost half of

157
00:05:21,181 --> 00:05:22,150
the capacity bill.

158
00:05:22,150 --> 00:05:24,017
And that bill is shared by everyone

159
00:05:24,017 --> 00:05:26,110
who uses power at the peak.

160
00:05:26,610 --> 00:05:30,715
Including Maya.

161
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:34,450
The monitor's proposed fix is blunt.

162
00:05:34,450 --> 00:05:36,320
Take the data centers out of the

163
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:37,338
shared auction,

164
00:05:37,338 --> 00:05:39,600
and make them buy their own power.

165
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:41,610
PJM is trying other routes,

166
00:05:41,610 --> 00:05:43,724
like letting big new users connect if

167
00:05:43,724 --> 00:05:45,415
they agree to cut back when the

168
00:05:45,415 --> 00:05:46,860
grid is stressed.

169
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:47,428
None of it has settled the real

170
00:05:47,428 --> 00:05:47,700
question.

171
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,793
Who pays?

172
00:05:54,250 --> 00:05:55,980
Now the wires.

173
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:57,489
Maya's utility,

174
00:05:57,489 --> 00:05:58,434
Dominion,

175
00:05:58,434 --> 00:06:00,614
and its neighbor AEP have filed plans

176
00:06:00,614 --> 00:06:02,551
to spend more than a hundred and

177
00:06:02,551 --> 00:06:04,973
thirty billion dollars by the end of

178
00:06:04,973 --> 00:06:06,300
the decade.

179
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:07,421
New lines,

180
00:06:07,421 --> 00:06:08,703
new substations,

181
00:06:08,703 --> 00:06:10,350
new power plants.

182
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:12,800
And both point to the same reason.

183
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:14,394
Giant new customers,

184
00:06:14,394 --> 00:06:16,886
above all data centers.

185
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:19,940
Dev builds those lines for a living.

186
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:20,829
Lately,

187
00:06:20,829 --> 00:06:21,490
he says,

188
00:06:21,490 --> 00:06:23,230
the work never stops.

189
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:24,269
Steel towers,

190
00:06:24,269 --> 00:06:25,308
thick cables,

191
00:06:25,308 --> 00:06:26,614
new substations,

192
00:06:26,614 --> 00:06:29,545
each one planned years ahead for customers

193
00:06:29,545 --> 00:06:32,010
who may or may not show up.

194
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:34,919
And every tower he raises goes onto

195
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:36,459
a ledger that someone,

196
00:06:36,459 --> 00:06:37,329
someday,

197
00:06:37,329 --> 00:06:38,822
has to pay off.

198
00:06:39,083 --> 00:06:42,343
Here's the part most people never see.

199
00:06:42,343 --> 00:06:43,723
Let me draw it.

200
00:06:43,853 --> 00:06:46,785
A regulated utility earns a set return

201
00:06:46,785 --> 00:06:48,203
on what it builds.

202
00:06:48,203 --> 00:06:49,025
Build more,

203
00:06:49,025 --> 00:06:51,043
and its earnings grow.

204
00:06:51,143 --> 00:06:53,296
Then the cost is split among groups

205
00:06:53,296 --> 00:06:54,903
of customers.

206
00:06:54,903 --> 00:06:55,670
Homes,

207
00:06:55,670 --> 00:06:56,173
shops,

208
00:06:56,173 --> 00:06:57,205
factories,

209
00:06:57,205 --> 00:06:58,843
data centers.

210
00:06:58,843 --> 00:07:00,916
And how it's split is a choice,

211
00:07:00,916 --> 00:07:02,727
made by regulators,

212
00:07:02,727 --> 00:07:04,376
with formulas.

213
00:07:04,617 --> 00:07:07,909
And Virginia's own numbers show why that

214
00:07:07,909 --> 00:07:09,497
choice matters.

215
00:07:09,557 --> 00:07:11,444
In Dominion's latest rate case,

216
00:07:11,444 --> 00:07:13,777
a cost study looked at the customers

217
00:07:13,777 --> 00:07:15,685
who would form the new data center

218
00:07:15,685 --> 00:07:16,497
class.

219
00:07:16,527 --> 00:07:18,396
On the big transmission lines,

220
00:07:18,396 --> 00:07:21,127
they earned the utility about a seventh

221
00:07:21,127 --> 00:07:23,437
of the return that other large customers

222
00:07:23,437 --> 00:07:24,247
did.

223
00:07:24,527 --> 00:07:25,529
In plain English,

224
00:07:25,529 --> 00:07:27,982
the biggest users were paying relatively

225
00:07:27,982 --> 00:07:30,217
little for the biggest lines.

226
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:32,713
The regulators say they'll look at that

227
00:07:32,713 --> 00:07:33,762
split next.

228
00:07:34,017 --> 00:07:35,287
And Maya?

229
00:07:35,337 --> 00:07:37,532
The same decision raised her base rates

230
00:07:37,532 --> 00:07:39,528
by about seven and a half percent

231
00:07:39,528 --> 00:07:40,527
this year.

232
00:07:40,877 --> 00:07:43,027
Not all of that is data centers.

233
00:07:43,027 --> 00:07:44,060
It covers storms,

234
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:45,094
old equipment,

235
00:07:45,094 --> 00:07:46,477
everything.

236
00:07:46,477 --> 00:07:48,644
But it shows where a building boom

237
00:07:48,644 --> 00:07:50,297
finally lands.

238
00:07:50,297 --> 00:07:52,192
On the monthly bill.

239
00:07:56,650 --> 00:07:59,510
But regulators are pushing back.

240
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:00,584
In Ohio,

241
00:08:00,584 --> 00:08:03,326
after the local utility paused new data

242
00:08:03,326 --> 00:08:05,069
center deals around Columbus,

243
00:08:05,069 --> 00:08:07,040
a new rule arrived.

244
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:09,136
Big new data centers must pay for

245
00:08:09,136 --> 00:08:11,173
at least eighty-five percent of the

246
00:08:11,173 --> 00:08:12,960
capacity they reserve,

247
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:14,747
whether they use it or not,

248
00:08:14,747 --> 00:08:15,800
for years.

249
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:17,176
Walk away early,

250
00:08:17,176 --> 00:08:19,220
and there's an exit fee.

251
00:08:19,483 --> 00:08:22,913
And these fights happen in public.

252
00:08:23,093 --> 00:08:25,481
When Virginia decided its rules,

253
00:08:25,481 --> 00:08:27,890
the parties in the room included lawyers

254
00:08:27,890 --> 00:08:28,964
for Amazon,

255
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:30,475
Google and Microsoft,

256
00:08:30,475 --> 00:08:32,425
the data center industry,

257
00:08:32,425 --> 00:08:34,155
consumer advocates,

258
00:08:34,155 --> 00:08:36,565
and the state's own attorney general's

259
00:08:36,565 --> 00:08:37,603
office.

260
00:08:37,783 --> 00:08:39,933
Anyone can read the filings.

261
00:08:39,933 --> 00:08:41,546
Most people never do,

262
00:08:41,546 --> 00:08:43,883
which is exactly why the outcome can

263
00:08:43,883 --> 00:08:45,885
surprise them on their bill.

264
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:47,968
Virginia followed,

265
00:08:47,968 --> 00:08:49,813
with a new rate class just for

266
00:08:49,813 --> 00:08:50,800
the giants.

267
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:52,230
Long contracts.

268
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:53,660
Minimum bills.

269
00:08:53,660 --> 00:08:54,860
Collateral.

270
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,600
Dominion says the goal is simple.

271
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:59,526
Stop one group of customers from

272
00:08:59,526 --> 00:09:01,811
subsidizing another.

273
00:09:02,050 --> 00:09:04,550
Why does a minimum bill matter so

274
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:05,400
much?

275
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:08,128
Picture a utility building a line for

276
00:09:08,128 --> 00:09:10,561
a data center that never shows up,

277
00:09:10,561 --> 00:09:12,124
or that shuts down when the AI

278
00:09:12,124 --> 00:09:13,170
boom cools.

279
00:09:13,330 --> 00:09:14,557
Without a contract,

280
00:09:14,557 --> 00:09:16,846
that line stays in everyone's rates for

281
00:09:16,846 --> 00:09:18,050
decades.

282
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:19,374
With a minimum bill,

283
00:09:19,374 --> 00:09:21,590
the data center pays either way.

284
00:09:21,590 --> 00:09:23,846
That one clause may decide more about

285
00:09:23,846 --> 00:09:26,452
Maya's future bill than any chip.

286
00:09:26,717 --> 00:09:28,970
And the tech giants are signing their

287
00:09:28,970 --> 00:09:30,697
own power deals.

288
00:09:30,797 --> 00:09:33,235
Microsoft is buying the output of a

289
00:09:33,235 --> 00:09:35,186
reactor being brought back to life at

290
00:09:35,186 --> 00:09:37,737
the old Three Mile Island plant.

291
00:09:37,977 --> 00:09:40,560
Meta signed a long-term deal that keeps

292
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,116
an Illinois nuclear plant running,

293
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:46,557
its owner says without ratepayer support.

294
00:09:46,847 --> 00:09:49,357
Amazon locked in nuclear power in

295
00:09:49,357 --> 00:09:52,467
Pennsylvania for well over a decade.

296
00:09:52,567 --> 00:09:54,611
Some deals add new power to the

297
00:09:54,611 --> 00:09:55,467
grid.

298
00:09:55,537 --> 00:09:59,028
Others reserve power that already exists.

299
00:09:59,283 --> 00:10:02,013
And there's a stronger counterpoint.

300
00:10:02,013 --> 00:10:04,204
A Berkeley Lab study found that in

301
00:10:04,204 --> 00:10:05,058
recent years,

302
00:10:05,058 --> 00:10:07,469
more demand has usually pushed prices

303
00:10:07,469 --> 00:10:07,885
down,

304
00:10:07,885 --> 00:10:08,923
not up.

305
00:10:09,223 --> 00:10:11,356
More customers share the fixed cost of

306
00:10:11,356 --> 00:10:12,383
the grid.

307
00:10:12,383 --> 00:10:14,423
It's not clear that will hold.

308
00:10:14,523 --> 00:10:16,283
But new supply is coming.

309
00:10:16,283 --> 00:10:18,303
The EIA expects a wave of new

310
00:10:18,303 --> 00:10:20,923
solar and wind this year and next.

311
00:10:20,993 --> 00:10:23,066
And PJM itself trimmed its near-term

312
00:10:23,066 --> 00:10:23,964
forecast,

313
00:10:23,964 --> 00:10:26,498
after checking data center requests more

314
00:10:26,498 --> 00:10:28,019
carefully.

315
00:10:32,483 --> 00:10:33,145
So,

316
00:10:33,145 --> 00:10:36,143
will AI make Maya's bill explode?

317
00:10:36,143 --> 00:10:37,113
Nationally,

318
00:10:37,113 --> 00:10:39,693
the government's own forecast says no.

319
00:10:39,713 --> 00:10:42,348
The average price for American homes is

320
00:10:42,348 --> 00:10:44,577
expected to climb about about thirteen

321
00:10:44,577 --> 00:10:46,393
percent over three years.

322
00:10:46,413 --> 00:10:47,563
Rising.

323
00:10:47,563 --> 00:10:49,569
Not exploding.

324
00:10:49,817 --> 00:10:52,667
But averages hide the hot spots.

325
00:10:52,737 --> 00:10:55,062
Where data centers cluster and the grid

326
00:10:55,062 --> 00:10:55,737
runs short,

327
00:10:55,737 --> 00:10:58,294
the capacity and wire slices are the

328
00:10:58,294 --> 00:11:00,057
ones that can jump.

329
00:11:00,317 --> 00:11:02,567
Whether they land on Maya comes down

330
00:11:02,567 --> 00:11:03,917
to three dials.

331
00:11:04,027 --> 00:11:04,477
First,

332
00:11:04,477 --> 00:11:07,061
do the giants sign minimum-bill contracts,

333
00:11:07,061 --> 00:11:09,557
so they carry their own grid costs?

334
00:11:09,677 --> 00:11:10,370
Second,

335
00:11:10,370 --> 00:11:12,520
does new supply arrive as fast as

336
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:13,837
new demand?

337
00:11:14,107 --> 00:11:14,528
Third,

338
00:11:14,528 --> 00:11:16,741
how do regulators split the cost of

339
00:11:16,741 --> 00:11:18,227
shared lines?

340
00:11:18,307 --> 00:11:19,917
Turn them all the right way,

341
00:11:19,917 --> 00:11:22,617
and growth can even spread costs thinner.

342
00:11:22,797 --> 00:11:24,248
Turn them the other way,

343
00:11:24,248 --> 00:11:26,141
and households help pay for the AI

344
00:11:26,141 --> 00:11:26,951
boom.

345
00:11:27,217 --> 00:11:27,871
For Maya,

346
00:11:27,871 --> 00:11:29,899
that means the most important part of

347
00:11:29,899 --> 00:11:32,047
her bill isn't printed on it.

348
00:11:32,047 --> 00:11:34,195
It's the contract the data center down

349
00:11:34,195 --> 00:11:35,247
the road signed,

350
00:11:35,247 --> 00:11:36,277
or didn't.

351
00:11:36,317 --> 00:11:38,292
It's whether the new power plants arrive

352
00:11:38,292 --> 00:11:39,207
on time.

353
00:11:39,207 --> 00:11:40,983
And it's a formula in a rate

354
00:11:40,983 --> 00:11:43,557
case she has probably never heard of.

355
00:11:43,817 --> 00:11:47,227
The AI boom's power bill is real.

356
00:11:47,247 --> 00:11:49,499
It's measured in billions of dollars and

357
00:11:49,499 --> 00:11:51,327
in whole power plants.

358
00:11:51,347 --> 00:11:53,540
But who pays it isn't decided by

359
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:54,617
physics.

360
00:11:54,617 --> 00:11:57,240
It's decided in auction rules and rate

361
00:11:57,240 --> 00:11:57,932
cases,

362
00:11:57,932 --> 00:12:00,847
in hearing rooms most people never watch.

363
00:12:00,847 --> 00:12:02,740
And that may be the most useful

364
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:04,842
thing to know about your next electric

365
00:12:04,842 --> 00:12:05,653
bill.

366
00:12:05,917 --> 00:12:06,577
Next time,

367
00:12:06,577 --> 00:12:09,536
we follow the money behind another hidden

368
00:12:09,536 --> 00:12:11,957
link of the world economy.

369
00:12:12,197 --> 00:12:14,500
This is The Hidden Economics.
